# ==============================================================================
# SENSOR-TO-ROBOT CALIBRATION -- where the Azure and the Keyence actually are
# ==============================================================================
# WHY THIS EXISTS. The Keyence raster is planned by Noether OVER THE AZURE'S
# MESH, so a sensor mounting error becomes a flight-height error for the whole
# scan. An uncalibrated Azure has been seen to put a surface-ground flat plate
# 13.6 mm low across the plate and 22 mm low at one end, against three
# measurements of the same plate:
#
#     contact touch-off   z = 0.9402      the robot's own tool tip
#     Keyence             z = 0.9409      +0.7 mm
#     Azure               z = 0.9273      -12.9 mm
#
# The LJ-V7080 measures over 0.057..0.103 m, so a 13.6 mm mesh error flies the
# head at about 58 mm against a 57 mm hard limit: a large fraction of the pass
# returns garbage and the probe comes close to contact.
#
# THE THREE THINGS THIS MODULE DOES, and they are not equally important:
#
#   calibrate_azure()       hand-eye: solve probe_end -> the Azure's optical
#                           frame from a printed checkerboard seen at many tool
#                           orientations. No touch-off anywhere in it.
#   calibrate_keyence()     solve probe_end -> the Keyence's optical frame by
#                           landing its profiles on a flat of known shape.
#   verify_calibration()    fly both sensors over one flat patch and make them
#                           agree. Two minutes, no artifact, no touch-off.
#
# THE THIRD ONE IS THE IMPORTANT ONE. A calibration nobody re-checks drifts
# back, and the Azure's fixture on this arm is physically loose.
# verify_calibration() returns the sensor-to-sensor disagreement directly and
# is cheap enough to run before every scan session. Run it. The two
# calibrate_*() routines are for when it fails.
#
# NO TOUCH-OFF IN THE ROUTINE PIPELINE. Hand-eye needs only that the target
# does not move, and the verification needs only that the two sensors see the
# same flat. A touch-off is used ONCE, during calibration, as the independent
# absolute check that hand-eye cannot provide for itself -- see the note on
# blind directions below.
#
# WHAT HAND-EYE CANNOT SEE, and why the touch-off earns its one appearance.
# AX = XB determines the sensor's offset only through the arm's ROTATIONS: an
# error that commutes with every motion in the pose set is invisible to it, and
# so is any error in the robot's own kinematics, because the solve is told the
# flange pose and believes it. The contact probe measures a point in the
# robot's own coordinates with no camera in the chain at all, which is exactly
# the independent statement that is missing. One touch-off at calibration time
# and none afterwards.
#
# THE CABLE ENVELOPE IS A HARD BOUND ON THE POSE SET. Hand-eye wants large,
# varied rotations, and this cell allows tool tilts no further than +/-40 deg
# about world x and +/-20 about world y, with a wrist_3 band that is 270 deg
# wide untilted and 75 deg wide at x -40 (measured; see cable_envelope.dpi).
# So the pose set is generated against the MEASURED band rather than against
# textbook advice: the roll budget at each tilt comes from wrist3_band_deg()
# for that tilt, every candidate is put through check_wrist3() before it is
# offered, and what survives is judged by check_calibration_poses() -- which
# reports whether the survivors still determine the answer -- before the arm
# moves at all.
#
# The arithmetic is in calibration_solve.py, which is numpy only and runs on the
# host; calibration_test.py tests it there. This file is the moving and the
# grabbing. The procedure an operator follows is
# ROS-Scanning/docs/calibration-procedure.md.

